{
  "brief": {
    "contract": "n is an integer in 3..5000. Return the prime strictly less than n. Return a plain Python int, no symbolic objects.",
    "documentation": "Return the largest prime smaller than n.\n\nNotes\n=====\n\nPotential primes are located at 6*j +/- 1. This\nproperty is used during searching.\n\n>>> from sympy import prevprime\n>>> [(i, prevprime(i)) for i in range(10, 15)]\n[(10, 7), (11, 7), (12, 11), (13, 11), (14, 13)]\n\nSee Also\n========\n\nnextprime : Return the ith prime greater than n\nprimerange : Generates all primes in a given range",
    "documentation_truncated": false,
    "group": "primes",
    "id": "sympy.prevprime",
    "library": "sympy",
    "name": "prevprime",
    "signature": "solve(n)",
    "version": "1.14.0"
  },
